=died1
#speaker: Apistos
Who was the hottest woman in Greek mythology?

Medusa... one look from her made guys rock hard.

#speaker: Athena
What am I supposed to do with this information?

#speaker: Apistos
It’s a joke! Do I need to draw you a diagram?

#speaker: Athena
Where do you even get this nonsense?

#speaker: Apistos
Hermes has a whole scrapbook you can win in the gambling slots! And not just jokes—there’s tons of sketches of everyone on Olympus, and beyond. Sure, I’d rather get something more valuable, but honestly, after an intense card battle, a reward like this is weirdly relaxing.

#speaker: Athena
Keep making excuses. You just want to justify wasting your money. And why did they even let Dionysus put that ridiculous horse in the games? It’s stupid and disrespectful to history.

#speaker: Apistos
Oh, come on! Sometimes stuff like this actually helps me unwind.

#speaker: Athena
Sounds like the excuses of someone with an uncontrolled gambling addiction.

#speaker: Apistos
You just don’t know how to have fun…

#speaker: Athena
I’m just being rational. All this nonsense with Dionysus’ idiot lottery and Hermes’ ridiculous jokes is a waste of money and nerves…

#speaker: Apistos
And here I thought gods had nerves of steel!

#speaker: Athena
Around you, any god—even the most stoic—would develop a nervous twitch.
->DONE
=died2
#speaker: Apistos
Maybe I should start my own joke collection too?

#speaker: Athena
Don’t even think about it.

#speaker: Apistos
Why not? Look how well it turned out for Hermes!

#speaker: Athena
Well?! He mocks the gods, twists history, and shows utter disrespect for everyone around him.

#speaker: Apistos
I like it… He has a special kind of humor. Not everyone gets it!

#speaker: Athena
Oh, really? I think you just share the same foolish sense of taste.

#speaker: Apistos
Maybe we do! Will you introduce us sometime? I’d love to confess my feelings to him…

#speaker: Athena
…What kind of feelings?

#speaker: Apistos
Admiration, obviously. What did you think?

#speaker: Athena
As if you didn’t know… Fine. I’ll introduce you to Hermes — if you win the tournament.

#speaker: Apistos
Then what are we waiting for?!

#speaker: Athena
Your sources of motivation perplex me...

#speaker: Apistos
It's not a 'what' — it's a 'who'. A god, to be specific. Hermes, to be very specific!
->DONE
=died3
#speaker: Apistos
I’m definitely in awe of Hermes—his outlook on life and his sense of humor.

#speaker: Athena
I can’t believe you just said that… It’s hard for me to even imagine anyone admiring him that much.

#speaker: Apistos
Love is blind, they say... So enlighten me then, why do thoughts about Hermes from my lips scare you so much?

#speaker: Athena
You don't want to know, my poor infatuated little fish. Anyone can break a little fish’s heart, but no one can put it back together again.

#speaker: Apistos
I can take it, I’m used to it. One very mean goddess keeps breaking my heart constantly.

#speaker: Athena
You know other goddesses?

#speaker: Apistos
What do you think?

#speaker: Athena
You little—! Are you talking about me?!

#speaker: Apistos
I was joking… probably… So what about Hermes?

#speaker: Athena
If he’s got a whole collection of silly sketches about the Olympians, then I have an entire anthology of absurd stories about him.

#speaker: Apistos
Oho-ho! Surprise me then! Convince me that my sincere admiration of Hermes is nothing but pure foolishness.

#speaker: Athena
Once, Apollo asked Hermes to deliver a message to a mortal tavern. He gave him his lyre and asked him to sing a heartfelt ballad of love to one of the patrons in honor of his celebration. Apollo even wrote a beautiful, genuine text about celestial devotion. He couldn’t appear in the tavern himself, so he begged Hermes to be his messenger just for one day. Hermes, always up for anything, agreed almost immediately. He looked over the text, grabbed the lyre, and set off.
It was a long journey, but he really wanted to unite two loving hearts. But on the way, a little tragedy struck—Hermes’ famous winged sandals failed him for the first time ever. He stumbled, fell, and couldn’t use them anymore, so he had to continue on foot. Because of that, the trip took longer than he had planned. Still, he finally arrived at the tavern and decided to make a grand entrance, bursting in with loud singing from the doorway! He opened the doors, played the lyre, and sang with all his might of Apollo’s pure and radiant love, spinning around the tavern, not noticing anything around him. When he finished, he stopped in the center, spread his arms, and finally looked over the patrons—trying to find the person for whom Apollo had written the song… but he couldn’t.
Then it dawned on him: the atmosphere in the tavern was heavy, gloomy, everyone was downcast. So he asked the tavern keeper what had happened. And it turned out…

#speaker: Apistos
Well?

#speaker: Athena
It turned out he had crashed a funeral ceremony. Because he took longer on the road, he missed the moment and stumbled into a very different event.

#speaker: Apistos
… He sang a heartfelt love ballad at someone’s funeral? For a person who wasn’t even there??

#speaker: Athena
Exactly.

#speaker: Apistos
AHAHAHAHAH!

#speaker: Athena
Apistos, this isn’t funny. Your Hermes disgraced himself for an entire age. The townsfolk disliked him for a long time afterward! And Apollo never trusted him with anything again.

#speaker: Apistos
Hahaha! Oh… honestly, if Hermes sang a ballad of love at my funeral, I’d rise from the dead!

#speaker: Athena
So you don’t think that’s shameful?

#speaker: Apistos
Of course it is, I don’t even know how he dared to face people after that… But now I’m an even bigger fan!

#speaker: Athena
Your tastes are strange indeed, but I suppose—to each their own…

#speaker: Apistos
Will you tell me more stories about Hermes?

#speaker: Athena
Of course—right after your win!
->DONE
->END